    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The code above is not wrong. It compiles, it runs, it stores every age correctly. The problem is what happens the moment you want to *do* anything with those ages."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Where it falls apart"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Suppose you want the average age. You would have to write out all 108 names in one enormous sum. Now a student joins, and you add `age109` — and every place you wrote that sum has to change."
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**A variable holds exactly one value.** More data always means more names.",
        "**The names are invented, not computed.** Your program cannot ask for \"the 57th one\" — it can only refer to `age57` because a human typed it.",
        "**Nothing connects them.** The language has no idea `age1` and `age2` are related; to it they are 108 unrelated boxes.",
        "**The size is frozen at the moment you write the code.** A hall of 109 students needs a code change, not just different input."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The real obstacle"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Look closely at the second point, because it is the one that matters. Every other problem follows from it."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "When you write `age57`, the number 57 is part of the **name**. It is spelling, not arithmetic. The program cannot compute a name, so it cannot loop over your data — and a loop is the only tool that lets a few lines of code handle any amount of input."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "So the thing we actually need is not \"a way to store lots of values\". It is **a way to select a value using a number the program can calculate**. Everything in the next lesson follows from that one requirement."
    }
  ],
